    "result_text": "There is only one test class in the suite, and it passes. The build is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented `capacityWarning` for the create-owner endpoint. The flag is `true` when the owner's city already holds **40\u201349** owners (approaching the capacity limit of 50); the existing hard rejection at 50 (via `EnsureCityCapacity`) is untouched.\n\nChanges:\n\n1. **New function step** `AssignCapacityWarning.java` \u2014 counts existing owners in the owner's city (case-insensitive, matching `EnsureCityCapacity`) and sets `capacityWarning = count >= 40 && count < 50`. It runs before `SaveOwner`, so the owner being created never counts itself.\n\n2. **Pipeline wiring** (`owners.POST.yml`) \u2014 inserted `assignCapacityWarning` after `assignBulkSignupWarning`, under the same `transaction` governance.\n\n3. **Owner model** \u2014 added the `capacityWarning` field (`@Column(name = \"capacity_warning\")`) with getter/setter.\n\n4. **Schemas** \u2014 added `capacity_warning BOOLEAN` to all four `schema.sql` files (hsqldb/h2/mysql/postgres); tests run on hsqldb with `ddl-auto=none`, so the column must exist in DDL.\n\n5. **OpenAPI spec** (`openapi.yml`) \u2014 added the read-only `capacityWarning` boolean to the owner schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes it and MapStruct maps it by name on GET responses.\n\n6. **OwnerMapper** \u2014 added `@Mapping(target = \"capacityWarning\", ignore = true)` to `toOwner(OwnerFieldsDto)`, consistent with the other server-derived flags.\n\nThe `AcceptanceTest` (fill a city to 40, then the next owner in it warns) and the full suite pass \u2014 `BUILD SUCCESS`.",
